{
  "body": "## What this is\n\nA staleness audit of the open backlog, the reopens it turned up, and the root-cause fix for why cards rot.\n\nScope was set by the operator: audit every open item **except** the #2445 delivery-machinery tree (224 items, excluded as the actively-churning area).\n\n## Method\n\n20 parallel reading agents verified each card's claims against live source in `web-everything`, `frontierui` and `plateau-app` — not against plausibility. Every `ALREADY-DONE` verdict then went through a second, **adversarial** pass whose job was to break the claim: extract the card's `## Done when` clauses verbatim, find the artifact for each, run the cited tests, and hunt for the clause that *isn't* satisfied.\n\nThat second pass rejected **7 of 32** done-claims (22%). Without it, seven cards would have closed over undelivered work.\n\n## Results\n\n| Verdict | Count |\n|---|---:|\n| OK | 176 |\n| STALE-INFO | 209 |\n| ALREADY-DONE claimed | 32 → **25 confirmed**, 7 rejected |\n| DEAD-REFS | 12 |\n| STALE-PREMISE | 6 |\n| SUPERSEDED | 2 |\n\nA second sweep covered all **2,734 resolved** cards, testing whether each `graduatedTo` names a path that exists. 100 were dead — but only **one** was a false resolve; 93 were relocation debris from three known moves, 4 renames, 2 delivered-then-deleted.\n\n## Backlog changes\n\n- **25 resolved**, every one through the declared `resolve` operation (see below).\n- **5 reopened**: #2756 (resolved over a Rust SSR subtree that does not exist — `frontierui` has zero `.rs` files and no such branch), #1010 and #1161 (acceptance clauses with no artifact anywhere after the WE-is-contract-only migration deleted them), and their umbrellas #1002 and #1038, which `check:standards` correctly refused as \"a resolved epic with 1 open child slice\".\n- **97 `graduatedTo` paths corrected** to live artifacts.\n- **#2096 re-prepped** (#1935 stale-prep rule) — its three defaults had *shipped* before ratification, so its greenfield premise was false. No ruling made; that stays the operator's.\n- **8 follow-ups filed.**\n\n## The root-cause fix\n\nThe four rot classes are one defect: **the citation gate resolves a reference's *container*, never its *content* — and only in one of the three repos.**\n\n`findDanglingLoci` (gate 5, #2821) asks two questions of `we:path:NNN`: does the file exist, and is `NNN <= EOF`. That is a bounds check. A file that *grows* therefore keeps every stale citation green — `docs/agent/platform-decisions.md` reached 4,138 lines, so cites written when it was ~600 are still \"in range\" and point at unrelated content. The largest rot class in the corpus produced zero gate signal.\n\nAdded to `scripts/lib/citation-check.mjs` + `scripts/check-standards.mjs`:\n\n- **`splitRepoRef` / `makeRepoResolver`** — cross-repo resolution, **detect-or-skip and fail-closed**: an absent sibling reports `no-repo` and is named as *skipped*, never counted present.\n- **Gate 5b `findDanglingSymbolAnchors`** — resolves `we:path#symbol`, the drift-immune form gate 5's own error message recommends and which had **no validator at all**. Word-boundary matched, so `foo` cannot satisfy a file holding only `fooBar`.\n- **Gate 5c `findDanglingGraduatedTargets`** — a resolved item's `graduatedTo` target must exist. **Verified by reproduction:** restoring #2756's original frontmatter makes the gate fire; reverting clears it.\n\nWired **outside** the Rust-port branch on purpose: `runWeScan('citation-check')` is verified byte-identical to the four existing gates, so folding a fifth into that path would mean it silently does not run whenever the port is active — the very hole being closed.\n\n### Deliberately not built\n\n**Gate 5d (`scope:` path existence) was implemented and removed — the invariant is unsound.** A greenfield item legitimately scopes files it is about to *create*; #2756's own `scopeRationale` says so. Enforced as written it fired on #3483, #3484, #3487 and #3323, all correct cards. The sound discriminator is existed-then-deleted vs never-existed, which needs full history the shallow clones lack. Re-scoped on its card rather than shipped as a noisy heuristic.\n\n## Skill/operation wiring (#3224)\n\n`DECLARED_HOMES` went from **5 of 17** operations to **7**. The scan can only fire for operations in that map, so the map's contents are the gate's reach — and `resolve` (15 raw calls) and `scaffold` (45, the most-invoked) were both unwatched. Both are non-delegating, unlike the `claim` negative control.\n\n**Both report clean**, and that is the honest result: the scanned skills already instruct `run.mjs`, and the remaining raw mentions are prose contrasts, which the scan correctly ignores (`HOME_MENTION` matches an invocation, not a bare reference). This buys regression coverage, not a backlog of findings.\n\n## Corrections made to this work's own method\n\nRecorded in the report's Part 4 rather than quietly fixed:\n\n1. The 25 resolves originally went through **raw `backlog.mjs resolve` calls** — the exact bypass `operations/resolve.mjs` exists to end, skipping four guards. Fixed by **replay, not assertion**: all 25 reverted to `active` and re-run through the operation with **no `--force` anywhere**, since a refusal was the signal wanted. All 25 passed, byte-identical — so the resolves were sound and only the route was wrong.\n2. Two cards were left resolved that are **not really done** (#1010, #1161), reopened as above.\n3. `resolve.mjs`'s header claimed a skill \"still instructs the raw command\"; it does not — the skills were rewired and the justification was not. Corrected.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `npm run check:standards` — **0 errors** (warnings unchanged from baseline).\n- Full suite — **90 files, 3,601 tests** green.\n- `run.mjs verify` — **\"verified green for ef74eb85\"**, no blocking findings.\n\n## Transport note\n\nThis PR was planned by `run.mjs open-pr` (`--ref=lane/audit-open-stories --mode=park --parkLabel=review:human --sha=ef74eb85 --require-verified`), which refused to submit because this host has no `gh` credential and instructed submitting the planned argv through a channel that has one. Only the transport differs from the operation's plan. The `review:human` park label still needs applying.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nhttps://claude.ai/code/session_01WZz4BXVdMwn7MzztGBJb92\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code/session_01WZz4BXVdMwn7MzztGBJb92)_",
